{"v":1,"accession":"PRJEB37919","title":"Fungal communities decline with urbanization - more in air than in soil","description":"Increasing evidence suggests that degradation of biodiversity in human populated areas is a threat for the ecosystem processes that are relevant for human well-being (Cardinale 2012). Fungi are a megadiverse kingdom that plays a key role in ecosystem processes and affects human well-being. How urbanization influences fungi has remained poorly understood, partially due to the methodological difficulties in comprehensively surveying fungi (Schmidt et al. 2017). Here we show that both aerial and soil fungal communities are greatly poorer in urban than in natural areas. Strikingly, a five-fold reduction in fungal DNA abundance took place in both air and soil samples already at one-kilometer scale when crossing the edge from natural to urban habitats. Furthermore, in the air, fungal diversity decreased with urbanization even more than in the soil. This result is counterintuitive as fungal spores are known to disperse over large distances (Finlay 2002). A large proportion of the fungi detectable in the air are specialized to natural habitats, whereas soil fungal communities comprise a large proportion of habitat generalists. The sensitivity of the aerial fungal community to anthropogenic disturbance makes this method a reliable and efficient bioindicator of ecosystem health in urban areas.","center":"Biodiversity Institute of Ontario","data_type":"Other","submission_date":"2020/04/29 00:00","sample_cols":["accession","title","tier","context","collection_date","collection_year","country","region","place_id","place","location_status","lat","lon","submitted_location","isolation_source","env_broad_scale","env_local_scale","env_medium","eligibility_reason"],"samples":[["SAMEA6797160","HEL-U1-160819-A","probable_urban","air_dust","2019-08-16",2019,"FI",null,null,null,"unresolved",null,null,"Finland",null,null,null,null,"urban context stated at BioProj